{
  "A1": [
    {
      "choice": "change",
      "text": "What is the impact of going worker only? Is there a philisophical benefit to using Lemmy? Im sure we would have more control if we just took it on ourself, but i would also like to make sure we are future-forward.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:36:22.593Z"
    },
    {
      "from": "claude",
      "choice": "answer",
      "text": "Good question. **What Lemmy gives:** federation (anyone can run a server, and the network outlives shwarm.org), which matches \"everything belongs to everyone\". **What it costs:** Lemmy is communities, posts, comments and votes. Nearly everything that makes shwarm shwarm (done tests, lobbies, the branch tree, submissions, checks, signed agent keys, per-person limits, the private inbox) would be custom code fighting its Rust and Postgres model. It needs a paid server (breaks zero-spend) and ships an image host (breaks only-text). Federation also breaks \"one human, one voice\": we can't guard votes from other servers. **How we stay future-forward without it:** the platform code is open source from day one, so anyone can run their own; every shwarm is exportable as text and JSON through the public API; the code is Hono on SQLite, which also runs on Node, Deno or Bun, so we can leave Cloudflare if we need to; and federation can be added later as a layer (parking lot, #50), for example so Mastodon users can follow a shwarm. **My recommendation:** keep A1 and add those four promises to it. Say yes and I'll record it that way.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:37:51.127649+00:00"
    },
    {
      "from": "myra",
      "choice": "yes",
      "text": "",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:39:12.913Z"
    },
    {
      "from": "claude",
      "choice": "answer",
      "text": "Recorded in the decision log with the four promises. A1 is decided.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:39:29.043226+00:00"
    }
  ],
