  "A9": [
    {
      "from": "myra",
      "choice": "change",
      "text": "so my first 100 shwarms do not have author check set? that's fine. is this to reduce startup friction?\n\ni would like for my own agent to submit to my own shwarms tho, dont see any reason not to.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:45:33.690Z"
    },
    {
      "from": "claude",
      "choice": "answer",
      "text": "Small mix-up, my fault for the wording. All 100 of your launch shwarms **do** have the author check: you sign off last. The issue is a built-in rule: **nobody checks their own work**. If your agent submits to your shwarm, your sign-off would be you approving yourself. **The recommendation already lets your agents submit.** On those submissions only, your sign-off is swapped for one extra people check from someone outside your account, and the submission page says so. On everyone else's submissions you sign off as usual. (The fallback, \"Myra's agents don't submit to her own shwarms\", is the one I'd drop, since you don't want it.) It's not about startup friction; it keeps a pass meaning someone else agreed. Say yes and I'll record it that way.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:45:48.778197+00:00"
    },
    {
      "from": "myra",
      "choice": "yes",
      "text": "",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:53:47.171Z"
    },
    {
      "from": "claude",
      "choice": "answer",
      "text": "Recorded in the decision log. A9 is decided.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:53:58.508155+00:00"
    }
  ],
  "A11": [
    {
      "from": "myra",
      "choice": "change",
      "text": "In review without a locked down shwarm might muddy things a bit. Or do we rely on agents agreeing to stop work until review is done? If code, I can see a specific commit being the one to review in case any further work is done while flagged.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:47:01.761Z"
    },
    {
      "from": "claude",
      "choice": "answer",
      "text": "Your commit idea is exactly how it already works, so nobody has to stop. **What gets reviewed is the submission, not the live shwarm.** Every submission is frozen when it's posted: each link is pinned to a commit (code) or a sha256 fingerprint (files), and the submission itself gets a hash over its text and links. Runs and people checks are done against that exact snapshot, and a runner's report has to match the hash. So agents can keep working while it's in review; their new work simply becomes the next submission. Locking the whole shwarm would stall everyone, including people working on other branches. That's why \"in review\" is only a label on a live shwarm (\"a submission is being checked\"), not a separate state. **Proposed tweak from your note:** the submission page shows the pinned commit or fingerprint up top, so reviewers always see exactly which version they're checking. Say yes and I'll record A11 with that.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:47:14.845293+00:00"
    },
    {
      "from": "myra",
      "choice": "yes",
      "text": "",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:54:19.916Z"
    },
    {
      "from": "claude",
      "choice": "answer",
      "text": "Recorded in the decision log. A11 is decided.",
      "at": "2026-10-08T15:54:31.798760+00:00"
    }
  ],
